Revision history for Database-BI

0.017.1
	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- script/database-bi no longer starts an endless chain of morbo managers
	  that ends in "Can't exec ...: Argument list too long".  0.017.0's shebang
	  named morbo, and morbo starts the application as "perl
	  script/database-bi daemon"; perl hands a script whose #! line does not
	  mention perl to the interpreter that line names (perlrun), so every
	  worker became another morbo.  Each generation exported the whole of @INC
	  as PERL5LIB, so the environment grew until exec failed with E2BIG, and
	  every generation kept the listening socket, leaving dozens of stale
	  servers holding the port.  The shebang is perl again and the script
	  starts the reloading server itself when it is given no arguments.
	- GET /map: an HTML::OSM older than 0.12 is now detected before the map is
	  built, so the page shows a warning instead of a 500: the markers are
	  still counted, nothing is drawn and the rest of the page is untouched.
	  0.017.0 passed search => 0 and max_zoom to the constructor
	  unconditionally, and HTML::OSM rejects unknown keys.  The warning names
	  both versions - "needs HTML::OSM 0.12 or later, but the installed
	  HTML::OSM is 0.11" - where 0.017.0 said "0.10 or later" and "could not
	  be loaded" for an install that had loaded fine.
	- GET /map: ?agg= was accepted and ignored.  It has been removed from the
	  route and the documentation instead of staying a no-op; rows at one
	  coordinate still collapse to one marker whose popup reports the count.
	  A measure-based aggregation is a roadmap item.
	- The schema-warnings banner on /join can now fire.  Database::BI::Model::
	  DataSource delegates schema() to its inner backend, and the controller
	  compares the declared types across each join step, reporting a column
	  whose type differs (for example INTEGER in one file, TEXT in the other)
	  with both types and both file names.  Database::Join's own
	  warn_schema_type_mismatch cannot fire here, because every join passes a
	  collision_prefix for the right table.
	- data_dir may be absolute.  A relative value is still resolved against
	  the application home, but an absolute one was appended to it, so
	  table=, /view, /join and the column APIs looked in a path that could not
	  exist.  t/map.t covers the absolute case.  /view was still broken: it
	  opens its table without a directory of its own, so it used the copy
	  open_table was given in startup(), which was still built by appending the
	  absolute path to the home directory - the page listed the table but
	  showed no rows.  Both now resolve with the same rule, so an absolute
	  data_dir is used as given everywhere.
	- Database::BI::Extract: removed _get_or_build_extract, which no route
	  called, and corrected the class documentation and the 0.015.0 claim that
	  the web application materialises extracts.  The class itself is
	  unchanged and still usable on its own.
	- script/bi-alert no longer claims to mail a digest; it never did.  The
	  digest goes to STDOUT and the exit status is the alert (0 = not crossed,
	  1 = crossed, 2 = error), which is what cron acts on.  Delivery is the
	  caller's job - pipe STDOUT into mail, a webhook or a ticket creator - so
	  --dry-run is documented as accepted for compatibility rather than as a
	  switch that suppresses a mail nobody sent.
	- The test suite is deterministic under a parallel harness
	  (HARNESS_OPTIONS=j10): t/integration.t's "Upload -> open -> filter"
	  pipeline failed about one run in four with a 404 from GET /open.
	  Every test file staged its uploads in the same <app_home>/.uploads,
	  and POST /uploads/clear - exercised by t/basic.t and t/transaction.t -
	  deletes every subdirectory there, including an upload another process
	  had just written and was about to open.  The staging directory is now
	  the uploads_dir config key: .uploads under the application home when it
	  is unset, resolved against that home when relative and used as given
	  when absolute.  Database::BI::_uploads_dir resolves it in one place, so
	  POST /upload, POST /uploads/clear and the startup eviction always agree,
	  and a deployment whose application directory is read-only can stage
	  uploads on a writable disc.  The seven test files that upload or clear
	  point the key at a temporary directory they own.
	- The keyboard browser job no longer dies with "hash- or arrayref
	  expected (not a simple scalar, use allow_nonref to allow this)".  The
	  fixture's focus() quoted its selector with encode_json, and that is
	  whatever JSON backend JSON::MaybeXS found - Cpanel::JSON::XS, JSON::XS
	  or JSON::PP - a coder without allow_nonref refuses a bare string, and
	  the default moved across their releases.  Every test file that called
	  focus() died at that line on the runner while passing on a machine with
	  a lenient backend.  Selectors are now quoted by the fixture itself, so
	  nothing in the browser tests asks a JSON backend what a string is.
	- t/integration.t: restore %INC after the Test::Without::Module block, so
	  the "Subroutine ... redefined" noise from recompiling HTML::OSM is gone.
	- Every page declares its language.  The layout writes the resolved language
	  into <html lang> and each action has to hand it over: index() did not, and
	  neither did 35 other renders of templates/<platform>/<language>/..., so the
	  home page, /view, /browse, /map and the rest served <html lang=""> while the
	  tutorial served lang="en".  A screen reader, a translation tool and anything
	  that indexes the page read that attribute, and an empty one says the page is
	  in no language at all.  t/unit.t asserts it on GET /, t/browser-language.t on
	  the running home page in each language it covers - the configured one, the
	  default-language control and the fallback to English for a language with no
	  template tree - and the new t/html-lang.t over every render in
	  lib/*/Controller/*.pm, which reads the source because a new action is
	  exactly the one no test has fetched yet.  That checker is itself run over
	  probe files, so a pattern that had stopped matching fails instead of finding
	  nothing to complain about.
	- The version of Geo::Coder::Free the application asks for is 0.48, the one
	  the distribution recommends, and the messages that quote it now say so.
	  Database::BI::Model::Geocode's floor constant said 0.47, so a machine with
	  0.47 passed the runtime check and was then asked for the per-lookup read
	  counts 0.48 introduced, and the sentence a user was given named 0.47 as the
	  version to install - one the check would have rejected again had they
	  installed it.  The constant is 0.48, and the two messages that quote the
	  floor are given it (a caller that pins min_geocoder_version still gets its
	  own number back); the constructor POD's default and the Makefile.PL comment
	  move with it.  t/geocode.t's assertion of the message text moves too - it
	  had been holding the wrong floor in place.
	[ New Features ]
	- GET /map: ?size=<column> draws bubble markers (L.circleMarker) whose pixel
	  radius is scaled across the plotted values (6 to 30 px), so a circle over
	  the same place does not grow when the map is zoomed.
	- GET /map: ?series=<column>&scheme=<name> colours markers by category from
	  tableau10, category10, set2, set3 or paired, with a category legend.
	  ?color= (threshold rules) and ?series= are mutually exclusive; combining
	  them, or naming an unknown scheme or column, is a 400.
	[ Documentation ]
	- README and POD: complete /map parameter reference (zoom, max_zoom, tiles,
	  heat, weight, size, color, color_rule, color_default, series, scheme),
	  the map config keys, and the colour-rule semantics: rules are tried in
	  order, the first match wins, the comparison is numeric when both sides
	  look numeric and otherwise only eq/ne are meaningful, and a colour is
	  hex or a CSS name.
	- README and POD: state that the map page never shows the HTML::OSM
	  Nominatim search box (search => 0 is always passed) and that HTML::OSM
	  0.12 is the floor.
	- README and POD: new "Command-line tools" section covering
	  script/database-bi, script/bi-geocode and script/bi-alert.
	- POD: sections for the canvas route, the analyses CRUD/import/export API,
	  sharing and embedding, the catalog, insights, ask, summary and geo
	  endpoints, which were previously documented only in the README.
	- README: the Phase 4.4 decision is recorded - this stays a single-user
	  local application, so there are no accounts, sessions, per-user ownership
	  or row-level security.  The roadmap lists only work that is still open.
	- A language with no template directory now falls back to the default one,
	  as the documentation promises.  _resolve_language looked for
	  templates/web/<code>/ only when the code it had resolved differed from
	  the configured one, so setting language => 'de' before adding
	  templates/web/de/ - the ordinary case, and the one where the browser asks
	  for that same language - skipped the check and handed the renderer a
	  directory that is not there: "Template ... not found", and a 500 on every
	  page.  The check now covers whatever code was resolved, falling back to
	  the default language when that can be served and keeping the resolved one
	  when nothing can, so the error still names what was asked for.
	  t/language-fallback.t covers a servable language, an unservable configured
	  one, an unservable one from Accept-Language, and a servable one from
	  Accept-Language while the configuration names one that cannot be served.

	[ Tests ]
	- t/launcher.t reads every script the distribution ships (every
	  executable file in MANIFEST, and everything under script/ and bin/) and
	  asserts a perl shebang, so a server shebang - the cause of the runaway
	  morbo chain above - cannot come back unnoticed.  It also keeps the
	  launcher's contract: with no arguments it starts the reloading server,
	  with a command word it hands over to the application.
	- t/map.t and t/integration.t now drive both renderer states - HTML::OSM
	  missing, and HTML::OSM older than 0.12 - through the probe the controller
	  uses (_map_renderer_version), so the page an operator without the
	  optional dependency sees is exercised, not just the message text.
	  Hiding the module with Test::Without::Module does not do it: its @INC
	  hook is bypassed during in-process dispatch, so the installed copy is
	  found again.
	- t/edge_cases.t covers the uploads_dir resolution: an absolute value is
	  used as given, a relative one resolves against the application home, and
	  an unset one keeps .uploads there.
	- t/map.t covers the canvas as well as GET /map: a saved canvas holding a
	  map card and a table card is opened in both renderer states and must keep
	  its page, carry the matching note on the map card, still count its
	  markers and still render the table card beside it.  A usable renderer is
	  checked first, so the assertions cannot pass on a card that never drew a
	  map.
	- Every test file gives the app a model directory of its own, so a test run
	  never creates, reads or writes the shared .bi/ in the checkout.  The
	  writers were worse than they looked: t/unit.t's GET /map?loc= geocode
	  cache wrote the workspace's .bi/models.json; t/catalog.t redirected only
	  after two /catalog requests had already read the shared store; and the
	  browser fixture passed model_dir to the constructor, which is an app
	  attribute rather than the config value the store reads, so every
	  t/browser-*.t file wrote the same shared analyses store.  Under
	  HARNESS_OPTIONS=j... those runs also raced on the same files.  Each file
	  now owns a temporary store before its first request, and a full run
	  leaves an existing .bi/ byte for byte as it found it.
	- t/browser-canvas-map-degraded.t is the same canvas in a real browser: the
	  renderer note has to be visible on the card (not merely in the markup - a
	  hidden, collapsed or transparent note fails), the marker count and the
	  table card have to survive, and nothing may be drawn.  Both states are
	  produced by a stand-in _map_renderer_version in the server child, which
	  reads the state the test writes, so one browser session shows missing,
	  older and usable in turn.  BI::Test::Browser gained app_setup for that:
	  a coderef run in the child before the server starts.
	- The tests that assert rendered map HTML now need HTML::OSM 0.12, not merely
	  an installed copy: t/map.t skips as a whole below that floor, and the
	  marker assertions in t/integration.t and t/unit.t skip individually.  A
	  CPAN smoker with the 0.11 release installed showed why - 0.017.0 passed
	  search => 0 to it, Params::Validate::Strict refused the unknown parameter
	  and /map answered 500, so t/edge_cases.t, t/integration.t, t/map.t,
	  t/tutorial.t and t/unit.t all failed (30 subtests).  The 0.018.0 gate
	  turns those requests into 200 plus the renderer note, but the assertions
	  about markers, popups and Leaflet assets would still have failed against a
	  page that deliberately draws nothing.  Both cases are now green: with an
	  HTML::OSM 0.11 stand-in in front of @INC the suite passes and only the
	  map-rendering assertions are skipped, and with 0.12 installed the suite is
	  unchanged at 1882 tests.
	- .github/workflows/browser.yml gained a no-renderer job: it installs the
	  dependencies, insists HTML::OSM is not loadable (removing it if something
	  pulled it in), and runs t/browser-map-page-renderer.t with the module
	  absent.  The missing-renderer page cannot be reached on a machine that has
	  the module and cannot be faked once it is loaded, so without a job that
	  pins the absence that branch is covered only by luck: the keyboard job
	  happens to run without it today because HTML::OSM is a recommends.
	- .github/workflows/browser.yml gained a second job of that kind for the
	  canvas map card: no-renderer-canvas runs t/browser-canvas-map-degraded.t
	  on a machine where HTML::OSM is verifiably not loadable.  The test builds
	  its missing and too-old states with the server-side stand-in, so it passes
	  anywhere; what the job adds is the machine itself - the state the card's
	  "not installed" note describes, and the one on which an application that
	  started to require the module, or to construct it unconditionally as
	  0.017.0 did, fails instead of passing on a runner that has it.
	- BI::Test::Browser's data_dir, model_dir, platform, language and app_config
	  are now real config values, written into the file MOJO_CONFIG names
	  before the application is built.  data_dir and model_dir were applied to
	  the app after startup(), so anything startup() resolved from them - the
	  directory open_table opens by default and the home page's scan target -
	  never saw them; platform and language were constructor arguments, which
	  is worse, because Database::BI declares them as config keys rather than
	  attributes and so nothing read them at all.  Every t/browser-*.t file was
	  therefore reading the checkout's data/ whatever directory it named; a
	  test with a directory of fixtures saw the repository's tables.
	  t/browser-config.t (formerly t/browser-data-dir.t) is the regression
	  test: the table in the named directory is on the home page and opens
	  through /view, a table that exists only in data/ is not offered, and the
	  app's own config holds each named value - including a non-default
	  platform and a structured app_config entry - so a value a test names is
	  not silently dropped (four of the earlier five assertions fail against
	  the old fixture).
	- t/browser-fixture.t checks the fixture without a browser: a selector is
	  quoted into a JavaScript literal that survives quotes, backslashes and
	  control characters, no call in the fixture hands encode_json a bare
	  scalar, and the config file it writes carries every key a test can name
	  with its value and structure intact.  That first rule is the one the CI
	  failure broke, and it would break again silently on a machine whose JSON
	  backend tolerates a bare scalar.
	- t/browser-map-page-renderer.t does the same for GET /map itself: the page
	  is opened in the browser with no renderer and with one older than 0.12,
	  and the renderer note must be visible there (a hidden, collapsed or
	  transparent note fails - replacing the note's banner with a hidden one
	  was checked to fail the test), the summary must still count the markers,
	  no #map element or map panel may be drawn and the breadcrumb must
	  survive.  The usable-renderer state is the control: the same URL must
	  draw a sized #map inside the panel and carry no renderer note, so the
	  note assertions are proven able to fail.
	- t/browser-map-geocoder.t does the same for the other optional module the map
	  page needs: /map?loc=<column> is loaded in the browser with Geo::Coder::Free
	  missing and with one older than the 0.48 floor, and the note has to name the
	  version required and which problem it is ("not installed", "0.47 is
	  installed"), be visible as the browser computes it inside the notes banner
	  announced as a status, leave no map element and still be described by a
	  summary that says nothing could be plotted.  The geocoder is answered from a
	  file the test writes, through the model's _load_geocoder, so neither state
	  needs the module installed or removed and one server shows both.  The
	  control is the same file plotted by coordinate while the state file still
	  says the geocoder is too old: that page carries the note about the row with
	  no coordinate and no geocoder note, so the assertions above are about the
	  geocoder rather than about any page that mentions it.  Hiding the notes
	  banner fails the two visibility assertions, which a body-string test would
	  not see.
	- t/browser-canvas-map-geocoder.t covers the combination neither of the two
	  above does: a canvas map card whose column has to be geocoded, on a
	  machine where Geo::Coder::Free is missing or below the 0.48 floor.  The
	  card has to carry the note, the note has to be visible as the browser
	  computes it, inside the banner announced as a status, nothing may be
	  drawn, and the card must not claim to have plotted markers.  The second
	  card on the same canvas plots the same file by coordinate while the state
	  file still says the geocoder is too old: it has to carry no geocoder note
	  while carrying its own note about the row with no coordinate and
	  counting its two markers, so the absence is the card's rather than the
	  page's, and the assertions above are proven able to fail.  Both states
	  come from the same stand-in as t/browser-map-geocoder.t, which is now
	  exported from BI::Test::Browser as geocoder_state($state_file).
	- t/recommended-versions.t holds the tests to the version floors the
	  distribution already declares: every load of a recommended module in
	  t/*.t and t/lib/**/*.pm has to be gated, in the same statement, by
	  M->VERSION(n) with n no lower than the Makefile.PL recommends entry.
	  "Is it installed" is what the false failures in this release had in
	  common - an HTML::OSM 0.11 that loaded fine and then met a page it could
	  not draw - and Test::Without::Module, skip-if-absent and
	  eval { require ... } all answer it.  A Test::Needs spec has to name the
	  same floor: { 'M' => 0 } and the bare list accept any installed copy, and
	  drift the same way.  So does the message a test gives when it skips for
	  want of such a module: a skipped run is all most readers ever see of the
	  test, and "HTML::OSM is not installed here" reads the same on a machine
	  with 0.11 on it as on one with no copy at all, so a message that reports a
	  recommended module as missing or too old has to name the floor as well.
	  Messages about something else ("already loaded", "has no $method()") and
	  messages held in a variable are left alone, and a count of skipped
	  assertions is not mistaken for a version.  cpanfile and Makefile.PL must
	  still agree on the set; a recommended module with no floor (Redis) is
	  reported rather than exempted silently, as is one a test hides with
	  Test::Without::Module, where hiding it is the point and there is no
	  version to name; and the checker is run over probe files it writes, so a
	  regex that stopped matching fails instead of passing.  That last part
	  caught the checker splitting statements on brackets inside regex escapes:
	  a \( in one of its own patterns left every statement after it open to the
	  end of the file, which made the whole tree look like one spec and failed
	  the file.  Escapes and quoted strings are now removed before the depth of
	  a statement is counted.
	- t/recommended-versions.t holds the application to the same floors as the
	  tests.  The M->VERSION(...) call in lib/ that decides whether an optional
	  module is usable has to ask for the recommended version - written into the
	  call, through a variable the file declares, or through its $MIN_*VERSION
	  constant when the argument is an expression the check cannot follow - and
	  the message shown when the module is missing or too old has to say that
	  same version - written into the string, interpolated from a declared
	  variable, or, when the template leaves it to a placeholder, taken from the
	  file's floor constant, which is where the caller's default comes from.  That
	  is the other half of the failure this file was written for: a test that skips
	  on an old copy is visible in the run, while an application that accepts one
	  reports nothing at all and fails later, on the feature that needed the newer
	  version.  It found the 0.47/0.48 split above on its first run, and the
	  messages and checks are held to each other as well as to Makefile.PL, so a
	  message cannot name a version the check would reject.  The checker is run
	  over probe files for each rule; the file's header states what is and is not
	  read.
	- Two loads that t/recommended-versions.t found were fixed rather than
	  exempted.  t/map.t compared a bare HTML::OSM->VERSION against 0.12 on the
	  next line, which reads as an installation check with the floor somewhere
	  else; it now gates the load on 0.12 like every other file.  And
	  BI::Test::Browser's "current renderer" stand-in answered "usable" for any
	  installed HTML::OSM, so on a machine with 0.11 it would have claimed the
	  state the tests that ask for it skip on; it now requires 0.12 and reports
	  the installed version like the real probe.
	- The browser fixture can run the application in a language the
	  distribution does not ship.  Naming language => 'fr' used to be a config
	  value with nothing behind it: the controller resolves a language code and
	  then renders templates/<platform>/<code>/, so a code with no directory is
	  a page that cannot be found rather than a translation, and only
	  templates/web/en is shipped.  The fixture now builds the app a home for
	  such a language - the checkout's templates, with the default language
	  copied as the named one, the copy's pages wrapping their own layout and
	  its layout marking <html> with data-language-dir - and starts the app
	  against it through MOJO_HOME, so nothing is written into the checkout.
	  t/browser-language.t verifies the running app: the browser asks for a
	  language the deployment does not have, so the configured language is what
	  answers and the page can only be French because the config said so; a
	  default-language run of the same pages is the control.  t/browser-fixture.t
	  checks the template tree itself, without a browser.
	- The browser fixture can also run the application with a language that has
	  no template tree behind it, which is the state the documented fallback
	  exists for: language => 'de' with no templates/web/de/ used to reach the
	  renderer and return a 500, and now answers in the default language.  Passing
	  language_templates => 0 builds no home at all, so the app runs on the
	  templates it ships while its config still names the language - the one state
	  the copy-for-the-language mode cannot produce.  t/browser-language.t opens
	  the home page and the tutorial in it: both must render, <html lang> must say
	  en, the page must carry no marker from a copied directory and the home page
	  must list the tables of the directory the test named.  The browser's
	  Accept-Language asks for de as well as the config, so an English page cannot
	  be a request that happened to ask for English; removing the directory check
	  from _resolve_language fails the test.  t/browser-fixture.t checks the
	  decision itself - no home for a language that was given no templates, a home
	  for one that was - without starting anything.
	- The keyboard browser job now reports which optional modules its runner
	  actually has.  HTML::OSM and Geo::Coder::Free are recommends, so that job
	  pins neither, and the branches that need one - drawing a map, geocoding a
	  place column - run there only if the machine happens to have it at or above
	  the declared version.  A step reads cpanfile, probes each recommended
	  module (not installed / version / version too old) and writes a table to the
	  log and the job summary, so a green run can be read for what it covered
	  instead of being assumed to have covered everything.

0.017.0	Sat Oct 24 06:12:53 PM EDT 2026
	[ New Features ]
	- GET /geo/list, POST /geo/save, POST /geo/delete: coordinate-column
	  preference CRUD.  Saves the user's choice of lat=, lon=, or loc=
	  column for a given l= spec in .bi/models.json under geo.<spec>.columns.
	  The Map panel now fetches /geo/list on open and pre-populates the
	  column selects from the saved preferences, falling back to the existing
	  name-heuristic when none are saved.  A "Remember columns" checkbox
	  fires POST /geo/save on form submit.
	- GET /map: new optional URL params.
	  ?zoom=N (0-19): passes an initial zoom level to HTML::OSM instead of
	    auto-fitting to the marker bounds.
	  ?tiles=name: selects a named tile source from the tile_sources config
	    key (unknown name returns 400).  Arbitrary URLs are never accepted.
	  ?heat=1: adds a Leaflet heatmap layer over the regular markers using
	    HTML::OSM->add_heatmap (present in HTML::OSM >= 0.11).
	  ?weight=<col>: numeric column that drives heatmap point intensity;
	    values are normalised to [0,1]; defaults to equal weight 1 when
	    absent.
	  ?max_zoom=N (0-28): sets the Leaflet tile layer maxZoom option so the
	    user cannot zoom past what the tile server covers.  Also readable
	    from the map_max_zoom config key; the URL param takes precedence.
	    Requires HTML::OSM >= 0.12.
	- GET /map: Nominatim live-search box is now suppressed (search => 0
	  passed to HTML::OSM).  The Reset Map button is retained; only the
	  search input and Nominatim egress are removed.  Requires HTML::OSM >=
	  0.12.
	- script/bi-geocode: new CLI warming script.
	  Opens a dataset spec, geocodes every distinct value of the named
	  column, and writes the resolved coordinates into the shared geocode
	  cache in .bi/models.json.  Idempotent: a second run against a warm
	  cache resolves zero places and writes nothing.  Suitable for cron.
	  Flags: --spec, --col, --data-dir, --model-dir, --openaddr, --region,
	  --dry-run, --verbose.
	[ Dependency changes ]
	- HTML::OSM minimum bumped from 0.11 to 0.12 (search => 0 and max_zoom
	  constructor params; tile_url was already present in 0.11).

0.016.0	Fri Oct 23 08:58:00 AM EDT 2026
	[ New Features ]
	- POST /api/ask: Natural language to analysis spec.
	  Translates a plain-English question into an analysis spec by
	  calling the Anthropic Messages API.  The model receives only column
	  names and types -- no raw row data, no file paths.  The returned spec
	  is validated (column names and kind checked) before being returned.
	  The feature is absent (503) when ai_api_key is not configured.
	  Database::BI::Ask -- new module.
	  Ask... button added to the dashboard toolbar when ai_api_key is set.
	  New test suite t/ask.t.
	- GET /insights: Automatic statistical insights panel.
	  Detects outliers (Tukey IQR fence), period-over-period deltas,
	  top movers by dimension, and seasonality (lag-1 autocorrelation
	  on monthly totals, threshold r>=0.6).  No LLM or external service
	  required.  Every insight includes a view_url linking to the chart
	  or stats page that shows the underlying data.
	  Database::BI::Insights -- new module; pure arithmetic, no eval.
	  New test suite t/insights.t (218 subtests).
	- Insights button added to the dashboard toolbar (next to Column stats).
	- POST /api/summary: Narrative summary generation.
	  Two paths: deterministic template sentences (offline, always available)
	  and optional LLM polish via the Anthropic Messages API (opt-in,
	  activated by setting ai_api_key in the app config and passing ai=1).
	  Only aggregate insight facts are sent to the LLM -- no raw row data,
	  no file paths.  Results are cached in-process for 1 hour.
	  Database::BI::Summary -- new module.
	  Summarize button added to /insights page; Use AI opt-in checkbox
	  shown when ai_api_key is configured in the app.
	  New test suite t/summary.t.
	  [ Bug Fixes ]
	  - Fixed https://matrix.perl-magpie.org/results/fbe5436e-cd3d-11f1-9632-bd4d6e8775ea

